Myth: Before memory began, a gardener named Theron tended the Garden of Seasons in perfect solitude. He kept each season locked in separate chambers—spring's blossoms, summer's warmth, autumn's harvest, winter's rest—never allowing them to touch the world beyond his walls.

A deer wandered into the garden, shivering and thin. "Kind Theron," she said, "the world outside is frozen and barren. Might you release one season to help us?"

"Impossible," Theron replied. "The seasons are volatile. Together, they would clash and destroy each other."

The deer visited daily, and Theron grew to cherish her presence. One day, seeing her weakness, he cracked open spring's door to offer her fresh grass. But the deer, in her hunger, pushed through, and all four chamber doors burst open at once.

The seasons tumbled out, colliding and swirling. Theron cried out in despair, certain they would annihilate each other. But instead, they began to dance—spring melting into summer, summer fading to autumn, autumn yielding to winter, winter awakening spring again.

The deer nuzzled Theron's hand. "See? They were never meant to be separate."

Now the seasons circle endlessly, each necessary, each beautiful, teaching us that harmony requires release, not control.
